injury-double-count.fixPlan:
In src/season.js expectedPoints, use a priced-absence share for players whose ESPN projection already contains a long absence. Conditions:
- the injury report has a returnDate later than ctx.today and is not season-ending;
- forecast.sources.espn exists (games are ESPN's);
- no game of this season has been played;
- forecast.games < 41, i.e. below the playRate floor .5*82. This marks a total ESPN already cut for a long absence: Butler 39, Sharpe 27, DiVincenzo 4, Moody 12 qualify, while fresh unpriced injuries (Claxton 69, Furphy 61, Gueye 70, Strawther 66) and the ambiguous Porzingis 59 do not.

For those players, compute after = the club calendar games dated >= returnDate. Use share = Math.min(.97, forecast.games/after) for the post-return chain instead of playRate's clamp(games/82, .5, .97), and keep injuryFactor as it is. The simplest way is an optional share override passed into playRate or computed in expectedPoints before chainCurve. Do not apply the .5 floor in this branch.

Everything else stays as it is. In particular, do not apply G/after to every reported player, because that over-values fresh injuries ESPN has not priced (Claxton would become a false injured target). Optionally record a flag such as availability.espnPricedAbsence in projection_model.apply, so the rule is visible in the data. Leave the in-season playRate spell handling alone: the backtest shows that excluding the spell over-corrects (+0.097 bias). Update the docs/ACCEPTANCE.md availability note at line 98, whose claim that the report is not a second penalty is inaccurate for priced ESPN projections.

playrate-clamp.reasoning:
The mechanism is real. src/season.js:94 sets prior=min(.97,max(.5,forecast.games/82)). That clamp was copied from scripts/inseason_calibration.py:68, but there the prior is the raw previous-season games count of players with 20+ games and 10+ mpg (prior_of returns None otherwise), so the clamp works as a crude regression to the mean. In production, forecast.games is already a fitted forecast (projection_model.py:587: model share*82, or ESPN's games). It is the same number that meanSeason=rate*games uses at draft time. Nothing makes the floor an owner decision: league-rules.json has nothing on it, ACCEPTANCE.md:98 only reports the calibration MAE, and the clamp arrived in #36 together with the calibration. The tests only check 78 games, which is inside the band.

Statistics: on the held-out 2025/26 model forecasts (484 players), forecasts under 41 games are close to unbiased: predicted 29.0, actual 31.2, bias -2.2 games. The 0.5 floor turns that into +9.9; for forecasts of 15-25 games the bias goes from -0.8 to +20.7. Lower floors are monotonically better (low-group MAE: floor 0 17.7, 0.3 18.6, 0.5 23.1). I also ran my own in-season sequence backtest (382 single-team players, prior weight k=20). With a floor, low-games players do worse at n=0 (MAE .289 vs .215, bias +.13 vs -.02 share) and at n<=20. From n=30 on, the clamp is slightly better (.175 vs .179), and it is better for early 'breakouts' (played at least 70% so far: .175 vs .197). The reason is that k=20, fitted on established players, is too sticky for low priors: k of about 5-10 is better with the model prior, and then the clamp/raw gap disappears. Over all cases and every k, raw is at least as good as the clamp.

The magnitude on today's data is real per player but does not reach decisions. 60 of 482 market players have forecast games under 41 and all of them get about 40 expected season games, up to +215 FP (Hepburn 16.1 games -> 40.1, 355 vs 140 FP). Lively 31.3 -> 40.1 (+123 FP). Undrafted rookies 17.1 -> 40 (+121 FP). None of the 60 is on an FBL roster. The best inflated season value (Butler 526, Lively 521) is still below the weakest rostered player (Burries, 633). The best inflated month value (Lively 112) is within 3 FP of the weakest rostered rookies, under the 15 FP threshold, and the rookie rule blocks that swap anyway. With the floor removed, I found zero changes in:
- the top-3 bestTransfers for month and season;
- marketAlerts;
- bestPackage for k=1..3 on both horizons;
- injuredTargets for all 8 teams;
- the verdicts of the 20 recorded transfers.
The cap at 0.97 matters at most -12 FP, for 3 unrostered players.

Caveats against the proposed fix:
(1) ESPN-origin injured players already have the absence inside forecast.games, and expectedPoints also zeroes them until their return date. Raw g/82 makes that double count worse in some cases: Sharpe forecast 27 -> 12.2 clamped -> 8.5 raw; DiVincenzo 4 -> 3.9 -> 0.5. It fixes others: Moody 12 -> 31 -> 9.3, Topic 25.9 -> 39.7 -> 25.1, Sorber 17.1 -> 39.5 -> 16.4. Dividing the prior by the available share of the remaining calendar brings every one of them back to the forecast, but it changes injuredTargets (Claxton for teams 6 and 7). It is a separate pre-existing defect.
(2) Mid-season, the floor partly masks the too-large k for low priors, so removing it ideally comes with a k refit, which is optional.

Severity is low: the displayed season and month expectations of fringe and rookie market players are 2-2.5x too high, but no advice changes on the current data. The auditors' 'high' and their decision-impact claims are overstated.

verdict-band.fixPlan:
In src/season.js transferVerdict, replace the line `const m=gain.month,s=gain.season,T=threshold;` and the verdict ternary with:
```
const T=threshold,ws=explainSwap(ctx,transfer.outId,transfer.inId,'season'),wm=explainSwap(ctx,transfer.outId,transfer.inId,'month');
const up=(g,w)=>g>=T&&w.confidence>=DOUBTFUL,dn=(g,w)=>g<=-T&&w.confidence<=1-DOUBTFUL;
const sU=up(gain.season,ws),mU=up(gain.month,wm),sD=dn(gain.season,ws),mD=dn(gain.month,wm);
const verdict=sU?(mU?'up':'long'):mU?(!sD?'up':'short'):mD||sD?'down':'even';
```
This keeps the season-first ordering, now probability-gated. DOUBTFUL is a module-level const evaluated before any call, so there is no TDZ problem. Return `confidence:{season:ws.confidence,month:wm.confidence}` alongside gain/verdict/label.

In scripts/notify-transfers.mjs (rival effect line ~113 and own effect line ~124), append the season confidence to the label, e.g. `${v.label} (${Math.round(v.confidence.season*100)}% за сезон)`.

Optional, same spirit: show 'Лучше было' only when best.gain.month - gain.month >= threshold.

Update ACCEPTANCE.md line 111 to say verdicts need the gain to be at least 15 FP and at least 60% likely on that horizon, and that the percentage is shown. Do not change the spread calibration in this fix.

